+  <!-- ============ 1 Problem ============ -->
+  <section id="problem">
+    <h2>1 · Problem</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>Two verified player-visible stalls: a life with no route yet, and a finished route that survives a new goal.</li>
+      <li>Four mechanisms compound them: cancel-on-intent, a rationed lattice A*, a scheduler with no priority for route-less seats, and dead seats that keep planning.</li>
+      <li>Planner workspace is sized to the full lattice for every seat: ~68 MB per seat on a giant map, ~2.2 GB at 32 seats.</li>
+      <li>The focused test suites are green because the liveness cases are uncovered, not because they pass.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p>The tick-by-tick body report<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-1" title="docs/reports/s2-cog-body-tick-2026-09-03.md">1</a></sup> found the symptom: Season 2 cogs stand still when they must walk. Players can see two cases. The code shows both.</p>
+
+    <p>The first case is a cog whose current life has no route. When the path length is zero, the waypoint function of the follower returns the position of the cog<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-2" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:687-693">2</a></sup>. As a result, the seat tick emits an empty movement mask and records the diagnostic state <code>bnsNoPath</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-3" title="src/shell/body.nim:1311-1330">3</a></sup>. The second case is a cog that reached the endpoint of an older route and then gets a new goal. The completed route stays loaded, and its cursor stays on the last point. That point is the position of the cog. The engine reports the state <code>bnsStalePath</code>, "walking an older route"<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-4" title="src/shell/body.nim:310">4</a></sup>, but the movement mask is zero.</p>
+
+    <h3>Four mechanisms make it worse</h3>
+    <p><strong>Order churn discards almost complete work.</strong> <code>setStandingIntent</code> cancels the pending plan on every new standing order<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-5" title="src/shell/body.nim:565-590 (cancel at :582-583)">5</a></sup>. That cancel occurs before the navigation waypoint code applies its two-cell goal hysteresis<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-6" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:726-732">6</a></sup>. Thus the hysteresis never protects a plan in flight.</p>
+    <p><strong>The planner rations its work across ticks.</strong> The planner is a resumable weighted A* on a 4 px lattice<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-7" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:15, :584-640">7</a></sup>. A pooled budget of 256 work units per configured seat per tick drives it<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-8" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:31, :572-628">8</a></sup>. On a giant pool map the lattice has 344,487 nodes. The task measured that a far route needs about 137k work units<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-9" title="Asana task 1218165906459726 and its comments (measurements, gates, decisions)">9</a></sup>. Under the share of one seat, that is hundreds of ticks.</p>
+    <p><strong>The scheduler has no priority.</strong> The round-robin over seats does not prefer seats that have no usable route<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-10" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:577-598">10</a></sup>.</p>
+    <p><strong>Dead seats continue to plan.</strong> Death only changes the <code>active</code> flag of the seat<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-11" title="src/shell/episode.nim:987-997; src/shell/body_nav.nim:305-306">11</a></sup>. The planning scheduler never reads that flag<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-12" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:572-619">12</a></sup>. Thus a dead seat can continue to spend work units that live seats need.</p>
+
+    <h3>Measurements</h3>
+    <p>The task measurements<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-9" title="Asana task 1218165906459726 and its comments (measurements, gates, decisions)">9</a></sup> come from an arm64 release build with 16 seats on battle-royale pool maps 0, 21, 42 and 63. Cold far routes complete in 10-21 scheduler ticks. When goals alternate for 32 ticks, the planner spends and discards all 131,072 permitted work units on every map. The zero-movement counts are 260/512, 286/512, 483/512 and 428/512 seat-ticks. A no-path seat at index 15 waited 6-11 ticks for its first planning visit. The peak process RSS was about 2.37 GB. The focused suites pass (<code>test_shell_body_nav</code> 20/20, <code>test_shell_body_seat</code> 22/22). Thus the liveness cases are not covered. They are not red.</p>
+
+    <h3>Why the memory is that large</h3>
+    <p>A giant map has 85,814 nav cells and 344,487 lattice nodes. <code>newBodyPlanner</code> sizes every workspace to the full lattice<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-13" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:252-270">13</a></sup>. <code>newBodyNavSystem</code> allocates one planner, one cache and one path buffer per seat<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-14" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:197-233">14</a></sup>. The table shows the per-seat total on a giant map.</p>
