+  <!-- ============ 2 What the design keeps ============ -->
+  <section id="keeps">
+    <h2>2 · What the design keeps from the existing code</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li><code>BodyMap</code> stays the immutable per-episode terrain layer, including rooms, chokes and goal validation.</li>
+      <li>The exact collision predicate, the cost model, the follower shape and the per-seat danger field are all retained unchanged.</li>
+      <li>The zone paint-arrival field and three reference PRs supply the hazard projection, the safety hints and the semantic target contract.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p><strong>BodyMap.</strong> <code>BodyMap</code> is already an immutable per-episode terrain layer. It holds 1 px wall and clearance rasters (a chessboard distance transform), pixel components, and the 8 px nav grid. On that grid, <code>NavCell = 8</code>, and a cell is walkable when the clearance at its center is more than <code>PlayerHalf</code> (6 px). It computes watershed rooms over clearance. Chokes sit at room contacts, and <code>buildTopology</code> removes chokes closer than 64 px (<code>GateSeparationPx</code>)<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-16" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:13 (NavCell), :20 (GateSeparationPx), :482-658 (buildTopology)">16</a></sup>. It also holds the cover atlas, the validator tables, and the home fields. <code>validateGoal</code> is the only constructor of <code>ValidatedGoal</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-17" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:397-412">17</a></sup>. One convention is important for the index build. <code>roomLabelAt</code> returns a <em>one</em>-based label, but <code>BodyRoom</code> and the <code>roomA</code>/<code>roomB</code> fields of <code>BodyChoke</code> are <em>zero</em>-based indices<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-18" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:190-192 (roomLabelAt), :194-205, :633-654 (zero-based room indices)">18</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The exact collision predicate.</strong> A move is legal when <code>canStand</code> and the pixel-exact <code>segmentClear</code> both pass. The current planner tests both for every lattice step<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-19" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:164-165 (canStand); src/shell/body_planner.nim:298-329 (segmentClear), :610-617 (edge legality)">19</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The cost model.</strong> The cost of a step is its length x (1 + profileDangerWeight x danger at the midpoint of the step). When the nav cell of the midpoint is the blocked-penalty cell, the cost is multiplied by 8 (<code>FollowBlockFactor</code>). The profile danger weights are cpDefault 1.0, cpCarrier 2.5 and cpHunter 0.25<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-20" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:18 (FollowBlockFactor), :618-628 (blocked cost), :88-92 (profile weights)">20</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The follower shape.</strong> The follower has a path buffer with a cursor. The waypoint advances when the cog is within one <code>NavCell</code>. The corridor is 20 px. The cog is stuck after 8 ticks. The blocked penalty lasts 96 ticks<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-21" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:38-43, :687-717">21</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The per-seat danger field</strong> and its rebuild, which occurs every 32 ticks with a stagger and uses at most eight visible enemies<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-22" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:30, :44, :440-485">22</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The zone paint-arrival field.</strong> The sim builds one <code>ZoneArrivalField</code> per episode. The field has two surfaces. The <code>damage</code> surface is not gated, and <code>zoneDamageByPaint</code> reads it. The <code>arrival</code> surface is gated by the render layer, and it can say "never" under wall art. <code>zonePaintedForDamageAt</code> compares <code>damage</code> with the elapsed clock of the schedule (<code>sim.tickCount - sim.gameStartTick</code>). The grid stride is 4 px (<code>ZoneFieldCellPx</code>). The sentinel is <code>ZoneNeverArrives = 0xFFFF</code>. <code>ensureZoneArrivalField</code> builds the field on first use<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-23" title="src/ctf/zone_field.nim:1593-1616 (two surfaces), :1762-1796 (zonePaintedForDamageAt), :65 (ZoneFieldCellPx), :263 (ZoneNeverArrives), :1644-1662 (ensureZoneArrivalField)">23</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>From the reference PRs.</strong> PR #408 gives four items: the conservative MIN projection of the 4 px field onto the 8 px nav grid, the finite hazard price (never a prune), the derated ETA speed of MaxSpeed x 3 / (MotionScale x 4) = 2.0625 px/tick, and the 48-tick safe-horizon bucket<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-24" title="PR #408 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/408">24</a></sup>. The top speed is 704/256 = 2.75 px/tick<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-25" title="src/ctf/sim_types.nim:514-518">25</a></sup>. PR #409 gives the fog-safe integer nav hints: ticks until paint here, ticks to safety, geodesic safe distance in px, and a retreat octant<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-26" title="PR #409 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/409">26</a></sup>. PR #410 gives <code>navigate_to</code> with <code>target_class = "zone_safe_ground"</code> and a <em>required</em> literal fallback point, which the engine resolves<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-27" title="PR #410 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/410">27</a></sup>.</p>
+  </section>
+
