+    <p><strong>Coverage proof.</strong> Rollout subtask 02 runs an offline, exhaustive proof on every supported map. Every standable pixel must have a legal pixel connector of at most 32 px to an anchored nav cell of the same pixel component. Two anchored cells must be connected in the graph exactly when their pixel components are equal. The probes already show that pool:0 splits one pixel component into two legal 8 px components (a 34-pixel pocket). Thus this is an invariant to prove, not a sample to hope for. If the proof fails on a map, the build adds sparse 4 px micro-corridor nodes where the proof fails, before qualification. The route corpus is the cost test only. It is never the topology proof.</p>
+
+    <h3 id="design-hazard">4.2 BodyHazardOverlay (episode, immutable) and BodySafeCache (episode, mutable, bounded)</h3>
+    <p><strong>Server seam.</strong> The seam is in <code>resetFirstLightForSim</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-32" title="src/ctf/server.nim:3857-3870">32</a></sup>, after <code>newBodyMap</code>. When <code>season2Shell and zoneDamageByPaint and zonePhases.len &gt; 0</code>, the server calls <code>ensureZoneArrivalField(sim)</code>, takes a snapshot of the <code>damage</code> surface (never <code>arrival</code>), the grid dimensions and the field fingerprint, builds the overlay, and then constructs the nav system. In all other cases the overlay is a typed <em>dark</em> state: there is no hazard term, the hints are absent, and class targets cannot resolve, so the literal fallback of the play stands. Nothing is built on first use on the play path.</p>
+    <p>The overlay is relative to the schedule and has no origin. <code>gameStartTick</code> is -1 until <code>startGame</code> enters Playing<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-33" title="src/ctf/sim.nim:1694-1696, :6679, :6750">33</a></sup>, and that occurs after the construction of FirstLight. Thus the overlay takes no snapshot of an origin. The server passes the live elapsed zone tick (the same <code>sim.tickCount - sim.gameStartTick</code> that every zone consumer reads) into the FirstLight step. The hazard ETA, the safe buckets and the hints all use that tick.</p>
+    <p><strong>Overlay data.</strong> <code>arrival: seq[uint16]</code> holds, per nav cell, the MIN over the 4 px source cells that the nav cell covers (the conservative projection of PR #408<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-24" title="PR #408 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/408">24</a></sup>). Per segment, <code>arrivalMin</code>/<code>arrivalMax</code> let the query skip segments that are provably zero-risk or provably saturated. Per room, <code>maxDamageArrival</code> treats never-arrives as infinity. Size: 172 KB giant / 686 KB colossal.</p>
+    <p><strong>Safe cache.</strong> The key is (overlay fingerprint, game generation, horizon bucket of 48 ticks). The cache is recomputed once per bucket roll, and it is re-keyed if the elapsed time moves backwards. The set of <em>dry</em> portal sides (arrival later than the bucket end plus the horizon) seeds one multi-source Dijkstra over the side graph with static lengths. The result is <code>safeDistPx[side]</code> and <code>safeNext[side]</code>. The per-room flag "contains a dry cell" comes from <code>maxDamageArrival</code> in O(rooms), which is 302 on colossal. This is microseconds of work. No full-grid flow field and no full-grid scan runs on a bucket roll. (PR #408 built a full field again every 48 ticks. That is rejected.)</p>
+    <p><strong>Per-seat safety answers</strong> obey the contract of PR #409<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-26" title="PR #409 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/409">26</a></sup>. The answers are integers, and they are fog-safe because the paint front is public. If the own cell of the cog is dry, ticks_to_safety is 0. If not, and the own room has dry cells, a bounded legal BFS from the cog (fixed cap, 512 cells) finds the nearest dry cell. In all other cases the answer is the best of (walked chain distance to side <em>s</em> + <code>safeDistPx[s]</code>) over the portal sides of the room. The direction is the first step of the winner, as an octant. Every resolved source must satisfy the dry predicate (asserted). If not, the answer is "unreachable", and the literal point of the play stands. The identity of a <code>zone_safe_ground</code> target is (class, resolved source cell). The bucket is cache provenance, not intent identity, so an unchanged target never causes route churn. Resolution runs before the arrived test in <code>seatTick</code>, so the effective goal is the class target, not the fallback.</p>
+
+    <h3 id="design-query">4.3 Live route query (synchronous, bounded, runs only when a request changes)</h3>
+    <p>The inputs are the seat position, the <code>ValidatedGoal</code>, the cost profile, the danger field of the seat, the blocked-penalty cell, the overlay, and the elapsed zone tick.</p>
+    <ol>
+      <li><strong>Endpoint attach, both ends.</strong> The candidate anchor cells are within a ring of at most 4 nav cells (32 px, the current <code>EndpointSnapPx</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-34" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:16 (EndpointSnapPx)">34</a></sup>), in fixed ring order. A candidate is accepted only if the exact pixel segment between the anchor and the endpoint passes <code>segmentClear</code>. No candidate is a typed failure. Then the follower keeps an advancing old route if it has one, or it steers. The route always ends at <code>goal.goalPoint</code> itself, as the path of the current planner does<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-35" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:666-676">35</a></sup>. The play path does not use <code>BodyMap.nearestWalkable</code>, whose ring has no bound<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-36" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:418-427 (nearestWalkable, unbounded ring)">36</a></sup>.</li>
+      <li><strong>Same-room candidates</strong> apply when the start and the goal share a room. Candidate (a) is the straight line, if every cell step along it is legal. Candidate (b) goes through the best portal side of the room. The query walks both next-hop chains (O(degree x chain) reads) and stays inside the room. Candidate (c) is a bounded weighted room-local A* over <code>legalMoves</code> in a shared generation-stamped workspace, with a <em>fixed</em> expansion cap. The cap is a constant derived from latency. 4,096 is the first hypothesis. The cap is never derived from room size, because rooms reach 70,041 cells. The query scores all candidates with the full cost model, and the cheapest wins. A cap hit only removes candidate (c).</li>
+      <li><strong>Side-graph A*.</strong> The start and the goal are virtual nodes. Exactly scored chains (danger, blocked cell, hazard advanced cell by cell) attach them to the portal sides of their rooms. The edge cost = static length + danger-weighted cost + blocked factor + hazard price. The heuristic is the exact integer octile distance, 181 x min(dx, dy) + 128 x (max(dx, dy) - min(dx, dy)), over <em>cell-index</em> deltas between anchors. That heuristic is admissible for the encoded metric. The golden oracle states this formula and every rounding rule, so the integer implementation and the reference cannot disagree by convention. Ties go to (cost, side index). A constant expansion cap of 4,096 pops, more than the side count of any supported map, only guards against bugs. The workspace arrays have the size of the side count, and all seats <em>share</em> them (queries are sequential). They have generation stamps, so the query clears nothing.</li>
+      <li><strong>Segment-cost memo.</strong> <em>One</em> shared, query-local memo keyed by edge ID holds the danger-weighted segment costs. Each directed edge has a 4 B value plus a 2 B generation stamp (27,460 x 6 B = 165 KB on colossal, counted in the retained shared total). Nothing is memoised per seat, and nothing is cleared per query. The blocked factor applies to segments whose bounding box contains the blocked cell and whose cell list contains it.</li>
+      <li><strong>Hazard price per edge.</strong> The query starts from the entry ETA. It advances the ETA along the cells of the segment by their 1/16 px lengths at 2.0625 px per tick (an integer ratio), and applies the finite ramp at each cell in traversal order. The per-segment extrema only skip segments that are provably zero or provably saturated. The price is finite. It is never a prune.</li>
+      <li><strong>Assemble and install.</strong> The query assembles the whole route descriptor in shared query scratch: the ordered edge IDs with direction, the copied start leg or local leg, the copied goal leg, and the exact endpoint connectors. It validates every leg. Only then does it copy the descriptor into the fixed buffers of the seat and change the route revision. The edge-descriptor buffer holds 4,096 entries. That bound is safe because a non-negative shortest path is simple, and activation asserts it. The start/local and goal leg buffers hold 512 cells each. Their size comes from the search cap and the measured maximum chain (213 cells giant, 436 colossal). A failed query never touches the active route.</li>
+    </ol>
+    <p><strong>Cost estimate on giant</strong>, to be replaced by the numbers of subtask 09: the attach step is at most 2 x 46 sides x at most 213 cells, about 20k cell reads. The A* is at most 738 sides and 8,748 directed edges with O(1) memoised edge cost. The segments that the search relaxes bound a cold memo fill. In the worst case that is every segment, 224,822 cell reads, about 0.2-0.4 ms. This is the one estimate that can put the 5 ms maximum at risk, if all 16 seats query cold in the same tick. Thus the benchmark includes exactly that burst, and the fallback is to sample every second cell.</p>
