+    <h3 id="design-follow">4.4 Following and immediate movement (every navigate tick)</h3>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Legs, not a map-sized path.</strong> The follower executes the legs of the descriptor. The start/local leg and the goal leg come from the copied buffers of the seat. The middle legs come from the index by (edge id, direction, offset). The crossings come from the stored crossing cells. The last leg is the pixel connector to the exact goal. The waypoint advance, corridor, stuck and blocked-penalty rules keep their current values.</li>
+      <li><strong>Follow-time smoothing.</strong> The follower looks ahead up to K = 6 cells along the current leg. It targets the farthest point whose exact pixel segment from the actual position of the cog passes <code>segmentClear</code>. This removes portal-point kinks and octile zig-zags, and it is what keeps short gate crossings under the 3 % gate. The corpus measures executed masks, so the smoothing is inside the measurement.</li>
+      <li><strong>Advancing route.</strong> A route advances when its next waypoint is farther than the arrive radius from the cog. An arrived endpoint is <em>not</em> an advancing route.</li>
+      <li><strong>Local steering</strong> runs when there is no advancing route (a failed query, or an exhausted route while the goal changed). It evaluates the eight octants in <code>Neighbors</code> order. The candidate is self + 8 px along the octant. It rejects the candidate if <code>canStand</code> fails, if pixel <code>segmentClear</code> fails, or if the candidate is the blocked-penalty cell. Score = distance-to-target reduction (target = the next waypoint if any, or the goal) minus profile-weighted danger minus hazard price, in integers. It selects the maximum, and ties go to octant order. If no candidate makes progress, the cog stands and records <code>bnsNoProgress</code>. The rule is O(8) and allocates nothing.</li>
+      <li><strong>Diagnostic states</strong> become: idle, following, steering, no-progress, arrived. <code>bnsStalePath</code> is removed. It can no longer claim motion while the cog is stationary.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <h3 id="design-lifecycle">4.5 Request lifecycle</h3>
+    <ul>
+      <li><code>setStandingIntent</code> no longer touches navigation. The cancel<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-5" title="src/shell/body.nim:565-590 (cancel at :582-583)">5</a></sup> and the goal pin both go, and the pins go with the route-field slots. The nav coordinator owns every request decision.</li>
+      <li><strong>Route anchor</strong> = the goal cell of the last <em>accepted</em> request, installed or failed. A query runs if and only if one of these conditions is true: there is no route in this life, the goal cell differs from the anchor by more than 2 cells (<code>ReplanGoalCells</code>) on an axis, the profile changed, the seat was stuck for 8 ticks, the goal moves and 12 ticks passed since the last query, or the current route is exhausted. Sub-threshold moves do not move the anchor, so cumulative one-cell drift causes a query in the end. Epoch and provenance changes are invisible to navigation.</li>
+      <li>Queries are synchronous. Thus there is no pending state and nothing to cancel, and the replacement is ready in the same call. After a failed query, the seat keeps the old route only while it advances. If not, the seat steers.</li>
+      <li><strong>Death</strong> (<code>resetAfterDeath</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-11" title="src/shell/episode.nim:987-997; src/shell/body_nav.nim:305-306">11</a></sup>) clears the route descriptors, the leg buffers, the cursor, the anchor, the revision, lastXy, stuckTicks, the blocked penalty, the last-query tick, the follow counters, the desired profile and moving flags, and the semantic target identity. <em>Then</em> it calls <code>setSeatActive(false)</code>. The danger rebuild already skips inactive seats<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-37" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:481-483">37</a></sup>, and an assertion makes sure that no query touches an inactive seat. The shared index, the overlay and the safe cache persist.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <h3 id="design-arith">4.6 Arithmetic and determinism</h3>
+    <p>Search costs are integers in 1/16 px. An orthogonal step is 128, and a diagonal step is 181, with the exact integer octile heuristic from §4.3. A Euclidean heuristic in these units is <em>not</em> admissible: 181/128 = 1.4140625 &lt; √2, and 53 diagonal steps already show the gap. The search samples danger from the existing float32 field and quantises it to 1/256 at the point of use. That is at most 0.49 % relative error on a carrier-profile path, and the report compares it with the unquantised oracle. Profile weights are integer ratios over 256 (256, 640, 64). The blocked factor 8 is exact. The hazard ramp is in integer ticks. Every rational product rounds to nearest. Totals are in checked int64. The cost-gate <em>oracle</em> stays the 4 px float formula of the task, and it scores the returned route. The gate must see the quantisation of the search, so the oracle never adopts it.</p>
+    <p>Route choice uses no clocks, threads, async work, pointer identity or hash-iteration order. Replays re-drive input masks, and no body_* module is in the wasm build (PR #408 showed this<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-24" title="PR #408 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/408">24</a></sup>). Thus archived replays stay valid. Movement masks do change. Thus a collision-free <code>GameVersion</code> (currently "52"<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-38" title="src/ctf/sim_types.nim:58 (GameVersion)">38</a></sup>), all nine replay fixtures<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-39" title="AGENTS.md:377-401 (replay fixtures)">39</a></sup> and the committed replay viewer are recorded again after the gates pass.</p>
+
+    <h3 id="design-deleted">4.7 Deleted</h3>
+    <p>The rework deletes <code>BodyPlanner</code> and its lattice workspace, <code>BodyFieldMinter</code>, the route-field slots in <code>BodySeatCache</code> (<code>MaxRouteFieldsPerSeat</code>, pins, <code>peekRouteDistance</code>), the plan and mint jobs, <code>ColdPlanBudgetPerTick</code>, <code>runPlanningTick</code>, <code>prewarmColdPlans</code>, the plan budget events and traces, the scheduler cursors, and <code>bnsStalePath</code>. The duck cache stays, because cover-hold owns it. Per-seat route-query state decreases from about 68 MB to about 12 KB (edge descriptors 8 KB, two 512-cell leg buffers 4 KB). The per-seat danger and visited rasters (0.7 MB giant, 2.75 MB colossal) do not change, and they are outside this task. A uint8 danger raster with a shared visited raster is a listed follow-up.</p>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- ============ 5 Decisions ============ -->
+  <section id="decisions">
+    <h2>5 · Decisions and rejected alternatives</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>A hierarchical room/portal index over <code>BodyMap</code>'s existing rooms and chokes, with 8 px cells and exact pixel endpoint connectors.</li>
+      <li>One static segment per portal pair, scored dynamically. A portal-side graph, so that crossings are always paid. Integer fixed-point costs.</li>
+      <li>Each record below names the decision, what lost, and why it lost.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>An HPA*-style room/portal index over BodyMap's rooms and chokes.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> JPS, because its benefit assumes uniform static costs, and here the costs are profile-weighted danger, blocked cells and zone time. Recast/Detour, which has the correct behavior model, but it is a C++ 3D navmesh and an FFI dependency for a deterministic integer-bitmap Nim sim. D* Lite, which targets edge costs that change, but here the topology is static and the goals churn. Continuum crowds, which amortise shared destinations, but the seats have distinct goals and danger fields. No maintained Nim package covers deterministic hierarchical game navigation. Also rejected: the resumable scheduler with priority for no-route seats. It still needs map-sized per-seat state, it still takes hundreds of ticks for far routes, and steering alone lets cogs walk greedily into dead ends for seconds.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>8 px cells with centre anchors and exact pixel endpoint connectors as the baseline.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected as baseline:</strong> a 4 px index, which needs 4x the field memory (about 76 MB on colossal, more than the limit). Per-cell sub-lattice anchors plus sparse 4 px micro-corridor nodes, which the Codex reviewer preferred. That option stays as the documented <em>contingency</em> if the corpus gate reports a missing route. The probes show that the spawn component keeps 98.7-99.3 % of standable pixels, and the connector covers the rest on the sampled maps.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>One static segment per portal pair, scored dynamically, plus bounded weighted refinement in the endpoint rooms.</strong> This is the explicit trade of the task: "dynamic global danger may select among precomputed structural route segments; the engine does not need the exact cell-by-cell dynamic optimum."</p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> <em>k</em> alternative segments per pair, which costs more memory with no evidence that it is necessary. The gate decides.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>One shared, query-local segment-cost memo keyed by edge ID.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> an eager per-seat refresh at every danger rebuild, which touches every segment every 32 ticks whether or not a query uses it (13,730 segments per seat per generation on colossal). A per-seat lazy memo, which is 13,730 x 4 B = 55 KB per seat on colossal, against the intent that per-seat query state stays small.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>A route is ordered edge IDs with direction over a portal-side graph, with legs executed from the index, endpoint legs copied into small fixed buffers, and the whole descriptor validated in shared scratch before an atomic copy.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> a per-seat flat cell buffer with prefix truncation, which adds a second query/install lifecycle for a case that "cannot occur". A prefix that is accepted in silence is not a route. An ordered <em>portal</em> list over collapsed portal nodes, which lets the search change rooms without payment for the crossing, and which cannot say which of two parallel segments won. Live descent on the static portal field for the start leg, which discards a weighted local result and reads a shared workspace that the next query overwrites.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>Follow-time smoothing with the exact pixel predicate from the cog's real position.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> query-time polyline smoothing, because the cog is rarely on the polyline, and a Bresenham walk over cells proves a staircase, not the chord.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>Semantic target identity is (class, source cell), not the bucket.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> the bucket in the identity, which forces a replan every 48 ticks with an unchanged target.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>The hazard overlay is immutable. The per-bucket safe results live in a separate bounded cache.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> one object holding both, which contradicts the ownership and lifecycle claims.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>Integer fixed-point costs (128/181) with the exact integer octile heuristic. uint8 next-hop portal fields.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> float costs, because tie outcomes drift across builds. A Euclidean heuristic in the same units, which is not admissible (181/128 &lt; √2). uint16 distance fields, which take twice the memory, and 1/16 px units overflow on a 436-cell chain. An oracle that shares the quantisation of the search, which hides exactly the error that the 3 % gate exists to catch.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>Zone data: consume <code>damage</code>, gate on <code>zoneDamageByPaint</code>, and pass the live elapsed zone tick into the step.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> the <code>arrival</code> surface, which gives false safe lanes under wall art. The absolute sim tick, because the schedule runs on elapsed ticks. A snapshot of <code>gameStartTick</code> at FirstLight construction, because it is -1 until <code>startGame</code>, and it changes between games.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="decision">
+      <span class="decision-label">Decision</span>
+      <p><strong>Room "contains dry cells" comes from a per-room <code>maxDamageArrival</code> computed once at overlay build.</strong></p>
+      <p><strong>Rejected:</strong> a linear pass over all nav cells on every bucket roll, which is 343,256 reads every 48 ticks on colossal for a value that never changes.</p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- ============ 6 Gates ============ -->
+  <section id="gates">
+    <h2>6 · Gates</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>The gates come from the task. The design must pass them, not redefine them.</li>
+      <li>Legality and reachability are absolute. Cost inflation, body time and activation are numeric thresholds, measured on the canonical linux/amd64 build.</li>
+      <li>Structural gates forbid any full-grid search on a play tick and any map-sized per-seat query state.</li>
+    </ul>
+
