+
+  <!-- ============ 7 Risks ============ -->
+  <section id="risks">
+    <h2>7 · Risks and mitigations</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>The latency risks are the cold memo burst and huge rooms. Both have a fixed-cap or sampling fallback that never raises the playing-tick cap.</li>
+      <li>The correctness risks are the static-segment trade, two-sided anchors, and clock or platform drift. Each fails loudly, or a gate bounds it.</li>
+      <li>The hazard model is conservative-late and single-label. Both are accepted with a named adversarial test.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <div class="callout callout-warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Risk</span>
+      <strong>Cold memo burst.</strong> All seats query in the same tick with empty memos. Mitigation: the benchmark includes exactly that case. The fallback is to sample every second segment cell (half the reads), or a per-tick memo-fill cap that degrades the remaining edges to static cost deterministically.
+    </div>
+    <div class="callout callout-warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Risk</span>
+      <strong>Rooms are huge (70k cells).</strong> The same-room path relies on the straight line, the via-portal candidate and a local A* with a fixed latency-derived cap, never a cap derived from room size. On a cap hit, the portal candidate's full inflation counts in its stratum, and failing the 10 % max means adding structural alternatives to the index rather than raising the playing-tick cap.
+    </div>
+    <div class="callout callout-warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Risk</span>
+      <strong>A static segment cannot dodge danger inside a room.</strong> This is the trade that the task accepts. Endpoint refinement and the cost gate bound it.
+    </div>
+    <div class="callout callout-warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Risk</span>
